  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nNow that the late evening and early overnight thunderstorm complex\nhas shifted southeast of the region, the northern half of the area \nis currently in a stable cold pool air mass, while the southern half\nis still considerable unstable. Thunderstorms are beginning to form\nalong a stalled boundary currently placed across northern Indiana. \nIf these thunderstorms attempt to move southeast, they will encounter\nthe cold pool air mass previously mentioned. These storms would\nstruggle to organize as a result. Trends will be monitored over the \nnext few hours, but this is first feature which could play an \nimportant role in today's significant wind threat potential. \n\nThe second feature is across southern Wisconsin and northern\nIllinois. Near the northern tip of the low-level jet, thunderstorms\nare beginning to develop. To the east and south of these storms,\nundisturbed energy remains. While this is ahead of the better forcing\nassociated with the shortwave, the available energy to the south\nwould give it a much better chance to evolve into a more organized\nstorm complex into the morning hours. \n\nIf these two features remain on the weaker side, confidence would\nbecome very high that the local area would see a widespread, \nsignificant damaging wind event later this afternoon and evening. The\nsecond feature has the better chance of organizing, therefore it \nwould likely have the biggest role to play in altering local impacts \nfrom the higher-end scenario. There is also the potential where this\nfeature and the main event combine as they approach the local area. \n\nAs SPC notes in their discussion, all environmental parameters are\nin place for a widespread, potentially significant damaging wind\nevent across the area. The timing is also optimal for the local area,\nmoving through during the peak of the diurnal instability (afternoon\ninto evening). Damaging wind gusts of 75+ mph are likely if a well-\norganized MCS can develop and then move through the area. The major\nlimiting factor would be any earlier development ahead of this main\nevent. Despite the focus being on the more widespread damaging wind\nevent, embedded tornadoes within the squall-line would also be\npossible, especially on the northern segment of the MCS. Large hail\ncould also occur in the deepest thunderstorms. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nThe best chance for flash flooding across the northern portion of \nthe area would be if multiple rounds of thunderstorms occur later \nthis morning and through the afternoon. Then, if an organized MCS \nmoves through, multiple inches would have accumulated over this \nregion. \n\nOf more increasing concern, after the MCS moves through, moisture\nwill continue to move into the region from the southwest. Back\nbuilding of thunderstorms along the stretched outflow will focus a\nflash flood threat over a portion of the area. This activity could\ncontinue well into Wednesday as the front stalls over southern\nportions of the area. SPC HREF LPMM does support this potential over\nsouth-central and southern Ohio, but there is a growing signal for\nsoutheast Indiana, southwest Ohio and northern Kentucky (some of\nthese counties are outside the flood watch). Because this threat may\nnot evolve until later tonight and into Wednesday, the flood watch \nhas not been extended to include these areas quite yet. However, it \nmay be needed later today as confidence increases. If a back-building\nscenario can develop, localized amounts of 5-7\" are plausible as seen\nfrom earlier events in Perry County OH this evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3) \nThursday and through the weekend, long-range guidance continues to\nsuggest an active pattern will continue over the Ohio Valley, but\nsome breaks are possible. This is largely due to a ridge of high \npressure over the middle and lower Mississippi River Valley feeding \nmoisture northward. The trough over southeast Canada is supplying a \nsteady amount of northwesterly upper level flow, increasing wind \nshear over the region. There are signs this may breakdown as the \nridge builds northward over the upper Midwest into the weekend. This \nwould bring a surge of lower PWATs southward reducing rainfall \nchances Friday into Saturday. Chances for thunderstorms increase to \nround out the weekend.",
